  "strategy": "1. Approach and rationale\nThe proposed approach combines a structured initialization with a tailored optimization strategy using the method of Lagrange multipliers and projected gradient descent. This method systematically enforces the equality constraint while directly minimizing the overlap objective. The approach leverages the mathematical structure of the problem to project onto the feasible set after each iteration, avoiding the need for constrained optimization solvers.\n\n2. Mathematical specification\n- Decision variables: h \u2208 [0,1]^n_points, normalized such that \u2211h = n_points / 2.\n- Objective: Minimize C\u2085 = max_k Corr(h, 1\u2212h, k) * (2 / n_points).\n- Constraint: \u2211h = n_points / 2 enforced via Lagrange multiplier.\n\n3. Implementation procedure\na. Initialization:\n   i. If initial_h_values exists, use as starting point.\n   ii. Else, create fallback h with two balanced rectangular pulses.\nb. Compute gradients:\n   i. For each h, compute Corr(h,1\u2212h).\n   ii. Identify maximum overlap lag k0.\n   iii. Compute gradient of overlap at k0 using finite differences.\nc. Descent step:\n   i. Update h using gradient descent with Lagrange multiplier.\n   ii. Project h onto [0,1]^n_points.\n   iii. Rescale h to ensure \u2211h = n_points / 2.\nd. Termination:\n   i. Stop when budget exhausted or C\u2085 improvement stagnates.\n   \n4. Search choices and useful variations\n- Line search: backtracking with 0.1 reduction factor.\n- Step sizes: start at \u03b7=0.01, decrease by factor of 0.5 after 200 steps.\n- Only project and rescale after descent step to maintain feasibility.\n\n5. Compute budget and robustness\n- Sparse gradient computations with early stopping.\n- Each iteration O(n_points^2) for correlation.\n- Budget: 500 iterations, 1s/iter for n_points=70.\n\n6. Validation and return contract\n- Final h must satisfy 0 \u2264 h[i] \u22641 and \u2211h =n_points/2.\n- Validate with evaluate_erdos_solution.\n- Return best h, its C\u2085, and n_points.",
